---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import InvestigationCycle from "../components/InvestigationCycle.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="subtitle">A forensic approach to understanding and repairing unfamiliar software.</p>

  <p>
    You have just been handed a system you did not build. Its author is not in
    the room. Something in it is failing, the documentation is partial or
    out of date, and the only way to find out what is actually wrong is to
    investigate it — read it, run it, question your own assumptions about it,
    and prove a diagnosis with evidence before you touch anything. That
    situation, not a topic on a syllabus, is what {courseMeta.code} teaches.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not a tour of debugging tools, and it is not a general software
    engineering survey organised by technology. Every system you meet here
    was built by someone else and is already running; nothing in this course
    asks you to build one from a blank slate. What is graded is the
    investigation itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Every exercise runs the same fixed sequence. What changes across the
    semester is not the sequence, but how much of it is scaffolded for you.
  </p>
  <InvestigationCycle />
  <p>
    Weeks 1–2 model this cycle closely. From there, scaffolding recedes:
    evidence sources widen, hypotheses start to compete with each other, and
    by the final weeks you are running the full cycle, unassisted, on a
    system you have never seen before.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Students who already write software competently but have found
    themselves — in an internship, a group project, an open-source
    contribution — responsible for code they did not write and do not yet
    understand. No specific language, framework, or tool is assumed; the bar
    is judgment and code literacy, not familiarity with a particular stack.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Course Overview" href="/course/">
      <p>The investigation method, learning outcomes, and who this course is for.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
